// std::convert — conversions the program supplies, as opposed to the ones the
// compiler already knows.
//
// `as` is the compiler's. It covers numbers, enums and pointers, with fixed
// meanings that a program cannot change or add to: `n as f64`, `flag as i32`,
// `p as *var u8`. Nothing you write ever alters what `as` means.
//
// `into` is yours. It dispatches through the `As` mark below, so a conversion
// between two of your own types reads the same way as a builtin one:
//
//     struct Celsius    { pub degrees: f64 }
//     struct Fahrenheit { pub degrees: f64 }
//
//     bind Celsius into Fahrenheit {
//         fn convert(&self) -> Fahrenheit {
//             Fahrenheit { degrees: self.degrees * 9.0 / 5.0 + 32.0 }
//         }
//     }
//
//     let f = c into Fahrenheit
//
// That binding is the same as `bind As<Fahrenheit> to Celsius`. The mark
// carries the destination as a type argument rather than fixing one, so a
// type may convert into as many others as it likes — each binding names its
// own target and they never collide:
//
//     bind Celsius into Kelvin { fn convert(&self) -> Kelvin { ... } }
//
// It works as a bound, too, which is how a function takes anything that can
// become what it needs:
//
//     fn report<T: convert::As<Fahrenheit>>(reading: T) {
//         io::println((reading into Fahrenheit).degrees)
//     }
//
// A conversion that can fail is not this. `As` promises an answer, so a
// parse, a narrowing or a lookup belongs in a function returning
// `Result<T, E>` — where the failure has somewhere to go.

/// How one type becomes another.
///
/// A binding names the destination, so one type may convert into as many
/// others as it likes. `bind Source into Target` is how it is written;
/// `value into Target` is the way to call it. `convert` can be called
/// directly, but the operator says what is happening.
pub mark As<Target> {
    /// This value, as a `Target`. Total: every input has an answer.
    fn convert(&self) -> Target
}
